even before the first ballot was cast  many political commentators have been  predicting victory for the ruling  liberal democratic party or ldp  it's ruled japan almost without  interruption since the end of the second  world war it enters the polls with high  approval rating and a fragmented  opposition is thought to offer little  competition  these polls take place just two days  after former prime minister shinzo abe  was assassinated in nara city while  campaigning  abe who was a member of the lower house  and head of the ldp's largest faction  still wielded considerable influence and  his assassination may result in sympathy  votes for the party  i think abe has thought a lot about this  country and i hope this election can  make his efforts worthwhile  there may be more people who will vote  for the liberal democratic party the  assassination was a shocking incident so  i think people have many thoughts but i  hope they can vote based on their own  decision  a surge of support will help prime  minister fumio kishida strengthen his  grip on power  generally speaking his polls are quite  high  but one thing which is dogging him is  the rising inflation and cost of living  as it is many in many nations of the  world if the the results tonight  are at or above expectations then i  think we can definitely say that kishi's  position will be secured for quite some  time going forward  it may even see the ldp and its junior  coalition partner komeito controlling  two-thirds of parliament which would  allow them to take the first steps in  amending the constitution  one of abe's goals had been to revise  the country's pacifist constitution  which forbids japan's offensive military  capabilities  meanwhile the suspect in the murder of  abe has been handed over to prosecutors  in nara where he will likely be detained  and questioned before being charged  police have acknowledged there were  security flaws in friday's incident and  have promised a thorough investigation 